   </property>
   <property name="text">
    <string>&lt;html&gt;&lt;head/&gt;&lt;body&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot; font-size:10pt; font-weight:700;&quot;&gt;Instructions:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot; font-size:10pt;&quot;&gt;&lt;br/&gt;Hover over the valve on the hanging molasses barrel to pour it into the fermentation barrel. Drag the Yeast into the same barrel. Once both are added, press the &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot; font-size:10pt; font-weight:700;&quot;&gt;Begin Fermentation&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot; font-size:10pt;&quot;&gt; button to start the process. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot; font-size:10pt; font-weight:700;&quot;&gt;Did you know?&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot; font-size:10pt;&quot;&gt;&lt;br/&gt;Fermentation is where alcohol is born. When yeast is added to molasses in an open-top fermentation barrel, the yeast begins to consume the sugars and convert them into alcohol and carbon dioxide. This step is crucial because it sets the foundation for the rum’s strength and flavor. In traditional Caribbean distilleries, open-air fermentation barrels are commonly used and often allow wild yeast from the environment to influence the rum’s character.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/body&gt;&lt;/html&gt;</string>
   </property>
